.TH TARIOND 8 "" "tariond" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
tariond \- Tarion directory server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tariond
[\fBserve\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B tariond
\fIcommand\fR [\fIargs\fR] [\fB\-data\fR \fIdir\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
tariond tells Tarion clients where to find each other and signals NAT hole punching. Chat messages go directly between clients and are end\-to\-end encrypted.
Two opt\-in features let messages pass through the server without it being able to read them: the relay (\fB\-relay\fR) and the mailbox (\fB\-mailbox\fR).
Accounts use argon2id password hashes; each device logs in with its own Ed25519 key, and a new device must be approved by one already on the account.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBserve\fR [options]
run the server (the default)
.TP
\fBinvite\fR [\-n N]
create one\-time invite codes (safe while the server runs)
.TP
\fBaccounts\fR 
list accounts, their first device, device count and bans
.TP
\fBban\fR <name|peer\-id>
ban a username or a device key (applied within seconds)
.TP
\fBunban\fR <name|peer\-id>
lift a ban
.TP
\fBdelete\fR <name>
delete an account and free the name
.TP
\fBdevices\fR <name>
list an account's devices
.TP
\fBdevices reset\fR <name>
forget all of an account's devices (the user lost them all); their next password login becomes the first
.TP
\fBid\fR 
print the server ID; clients pin it, give it to users as server_id
Operator commands take the same \fB\-data\fR (or \fBTARIOND_DATA\fR) as the server and write request files a running server picks up.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-argon\-memory\fR (default 64)
argon2id memory per hash, MiB
.TP
\fB\-argon\-time\fR (default 3)
argon2id passes
.TP
\fB\-bans\fR
ban list (default <data>/bans.txt)
.TP
\fB\-data\fR (default \&.)
folder for all server files (env TARIOND_DATA)
.TP
\fB\-id\fR
print the server ID and exit (same as `tariond id`)
.TP
\fB\-invites\fR
invite code folder (default <data>/invites)
.TP
\fB\-key\fR
server identity key (default <data>/tariond.key)
.TP
\fB\-legacy\-users\fR
old users.db to import once (default <data>/users.db)
.TP
\fB\-mailbox\fR
hold sealed letters for offline users who turn their mailbox on (the server can't read them)
.TP
\fB\-mailbox\-days\fR (default 7)
delete letters nobody fetched after this many days
.TP
\fB\-min\-password\fR (default 10)
minimum password length for new accounts
.TP
\fB\-port\fR (default 63425)
UDP port to listen on
.TP
\fB\-registration\fR (default open)
who may create accounts: open, invite or closed
.TP
\fB\-relay\fR
relay encrypted packets between peers whose NATs block direct connections (both peers must also turn it on)
.TP
\fB\-relay\-kbps\fR (default 256)
relay bandwidth per session, KiB/s
.TP
\fB\-relay\-total\-kbps\fR (default 8192)
relay bandwidth for all sessions, KiB/s
.TP
\fB\-users\fR
account database (default <data>/users.json)
.SH FILES
Everything lives in the data folder (\fB\-data\fR, \fBTARIOND_DATA\fR, default the current directory).
.TP
\fItariond.key\fR
the server identity; back it up: losing it makes every client refuse the server
.TP
\fIusers.json\fR
accounts, password hashes and device chains; back it up
.TP
\fIinvites/\fR
one file per unused invite code
.TP
\fIbans.txt\fR
banned usernames and peer IDs, one per line, re\-read when changed
.TP
\fIdeletions/, device\-resets/\fR
operator requests waiting for the server
.TP
\fImailbox/\fR
with \-mailbox: sealed letters and each device's mailbox key
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBTARIOND_DATA\fR
the data folder, if \fB\-data\fR isn't given
.SH NETWORK
Open UDP 63425 (\fB\-port\fR) inbound. Probes for clients' forwarded ports go out from a second, random UDP port.
quic\-go may warn that it can't raise the UDP buffer size; on Linux raise the limits with sysctl net.core.rmem_max=7500000 net.core.wmem_max=7500000.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 on an error, printed to standard error.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBtarion\fR(1), docs/operator.md, https://github.com/necromancer124/tarion
